      ":question/math-academy-id": "e-143",
      ":question/problem": "What is the slope of the tangent to the curve $y = x^4 + 3\\sqrt [3]{x^2}$ at the point $(1,4)?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "This time, we'll use $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$ notation. First, we compute the derivative, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4}+3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4}+3{x}^{2/3}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot {x}^{3}+3\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {x}^{-1/3} \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}+2{x}^{-1/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & =4(1{)}^{3}+2(1{)}^{-1/3} \\\\  & =4+2 \\\\  & =6\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve is $6.$"
    },
