        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-304130",
        ":question/problem": "Find the exact value of the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y=4x-2\\sqrt{x}$ at the point $(4,12).$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "This time, we'll use $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ notation. First, we compute the derivative, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x-2\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x-2{x}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =4-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =4-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=4$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=4} & =4-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4}} \\\\  & =4-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point $(4,12)$ is\n\n$$\nm={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=4}=\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$"
